## Reference conventions

{% include 'reference-conventions.md' %}

## What to populate

- `sections` — per reference-conventions.md. Each section is
  a rule category or band of guidance.
- `files` — usually empty (rules live in SKILL.md). Append
  only when a rule body is too long for SKILL.md or has
  independent reuse value.
- `rationale` — at most ~5 sentences or bullets: decisions
  made (rule scope, include vs exclude, prescriptiveness) and
  alternatives rejected. Nothing else.

## Output construction

```bash
LOOM=~/.kiro/skills/home/loom/scripts/loom.sh
WD={{ run.workdir }}

$LOOM output init "$WD" --task design-author

$LOOM output add "$WD" --task design-author \
    --set frontmatter.name=demo \
    --set frontmatter.type=reference \
    --set 'frontmatter.description=...' \
    --set 'sections.0.heading=Required Behaviour' \
    --set 'sections.0.outline=...' \
    --set 'sections.1.heading=Forbidden Without Proof' \
    --set 'sections.1.outline=...' \
    --set rationale='Rule scope and prescriptiveness tradeoffs...'
```

Syntax: arrays use dotted numeric indices (`files.0.path`,
`files.1.path`); `.` nests. The writer validates the whole
document after every `output add`, so required sibling fields
(e.g. `path` + `purpose` + `content_outline` of one file) MUST
land in the same call.
